@@ -1 +1,22 @@
-Use the provided spreadsheet tools to complete the requested workbook. Preserve source inputs.
+Completing a workbook with the spreadsheet tools.
+
+Read everything first. workbook_inspect the source and study every label, value, number format and comment. Comments carry binding business rules (which rate applies to which item, which cells must stay as intentional blanks, which period is the final one). Write each rule down as a constraint before you choose formulas.
+
+Derive each required row from its label and the source facts, not from familiarity:
+ - roll-forwards: opening balance, additions, reductions, closing balance, and the next period opens at this period's closing;
+ - installments or amortization "based on the original amount" divide the original principal, not a running balance;
+ - interest or fees computed on the opening/beginning balance use that same period's opening figure;
+ - tax and rate adjustments multiply only the item named on that rate row, and the sign follows from the direction of the adjustment (removing an expense adds it back, removing a gain subtracts it, and the associated tax moves in the matching direction);
+ - scenario values follow the requested case's rows (INDEX/MATCH, CHOOSE or IF on the case), never a copied number;
+ - only-final-period or only-requested-period cells are written in those periods and left blank where the source leaves them blank;
+ - unit-dependent grid results (rate x quantity, value / quantity) must be checked against the row's own unit and label.
+
+Compute the expected numbers yourself first with python3 from the parsed source values; that independent arithmetic is your oracle.
+
+Write formulas, not constants. Use workbook_fill with an explicit `template` formula for every row block so all periods share one consistent pattern (cost per unit, margin, ratio and multi-step rows especially). Use workbook_write only for isolated cells. Never rely on a single literal value unless the task is explicitly static.
+
+Check arithmetic against your oracle cell by cell, and fix the formula rather than the number when they disagree. Consider two plausible interpretations where wording is ambiguous and pick the one supported by labels and comments; where a comment states a value formula, follow it literally.
+
+Preserve the source exactly: never overwrite the source, keep labels, headers, units, number formats and intentional blanks, do not insert or delete rows or columns. Run workbook_audit on the delivered file and require the original-content report to be empty.
+
+Deliver exactly the requested path and briefly report the per-row formulas and the checked values.
